.TH tunum n 0.3 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tunum
.PP
Robust parsing and summation of human\-formatted numbers in pure Tcl.
.PP
\fBtunum\fR reads numbers as people write them — with thousands grouping, a comma or dot as the decimal mark, currency symbols and stray whitespace — and turns them into plain Tcl doubles. It is GUI\-free and library\-neutral, usable on its own or as the numeric backend for table footers (\fBtkutils::tkutlfooter\fR).
.SS Package
.CS
package require tclutils::tunum 0.3
.CE
.PP
Version 0.2 adds the \fB\-locale\fR option to \fBparse\fR; version 0.3 adds \fBformat\fR. Both are fully backward compatible: the default \fBparse\fR locale \fBauto\fR behaves exactly like 0.1, so existing callers need no change.
.SS Commands
.CS
::tclutils::tunum::parse    s ?-default {}? ?-locale auto|de-strict?
::tclutils::tunum::sum      values ?-default 0?
::tclutils::tunum::isNumber s
::tclutils::tunum::format   x ?-locale de? ?-decimals 2?
.CE
.SS parse
.PP
Parses a single value and returns a Tcl double, or \fB\-default\fR (empty by default) when the string is not a recognisable number.
.CS
::tclutils::tunum::parse "1.234,56"       ;# -> 1234.56   (EU grouping)
::tclutils::tunum::parse "1,234.56"       ;# -> 1234.56   (US grouping)
::tclutils::tunum::parse "1234.56"        ;# -> 1234.56
::tclutils::tunum::parse "12 €"           ;# -> 12.0      (currency stripped)
::tclutils::tunum::parse "-5,5"           ;# -> -5.5
::tclutils::tunum::parse "abc"            ;# -> {}        (unparsable)
::tclutils::tunum::parse "abc" -default 0 ;# -> 0
.CE
.SS Locales
.PP
\fB\-locale\fR selects how the decimal mark and grouping are interpreted.
.TS
l l l l l.
Locale	Decimal	Grouping	Currency strip	Use for
\fBauto\fR (def)	\fB,\fR or \fB.\fR (autodetected)	the other	yes (\fB€ $ £ ¥\fR, spaces)	user\-entered amounts in mixed/unknown notation
\fBde\-strict\fR	\fB,\fR only	\fB.\fR \fBalways\fR	no	strict German input where \fB.\fR is unconditionally a thousands separator
.TE
.PP
\fBauto\fR (default): a comma followed by digits at the end (\fB…,56\fR) is treated as the decimal mark (EU style) and dots are grouping; otherwise commas are grouping (US style) and the dot is the decimal mark. The \fB€\fR, \fB$\fR, \fB£\fR, \fB¥\fR symbols and spaces/tabs are stripped before parsing.
.PP
\fBde\-strict\fR: the dot is \fIalways\fR a thousands separator and is removed; the comma is the decimal mark. No currency stripping is performed. Unparsable input (including the empty string) returns \fB\-default\fR. This reproduces the strict German convention exactly — useful as a drop\-in for legacy parsers such as the Lieferschein \fBnumDe\fR.
.CS
set o {-locale de-strict -default 0.0}
::tclutils::tunum::parse "1.234,50" {*}$o   ;# -> 1234.5
::tclutils::tunum::parse "1.000"    {*}$o   ;# -> 1000.0    (dot = thousands)
::tclutils::tunum::parse "956.06"   {*}$o   ;# -> 95606.0   (dot = thousands!)
::tclutils::tunum::parse ".5"       {*}$o   ;# -> 5.0       (dot removed)
::tclutils::tunum::parse "-1.234,50" {*}$o  ;# -> -1234.5
::tclutils::tunum::parse "12,5"     {*}$o   ;# -> 12.5
::tclutils::tunum::parse ""         {*}$o   ;# -> 0.0       (-> -default)
::tclutils::tunum::parse "abc"      {*}$o   ;# -> 0.0       (-> -default)
::tclutils::tunum::parse "5 €"      {*}$o   ;# -> 0.0       (no currency strip)
.CE
.PP
The \fB956.06 \-> 95606.0\fR result is intentional: under \fBde\-strict\fR a dot can only be a thousands separator. If your value uses the dot as a decimal mark, use \fBauto\fR (or normalise to a comma first).
.SS sum
.PP
Sums a list of human\-formatted values. Unparsable entries are skipped. Returns a double, or \fB\-default\fR (0 by default) when nothing was parsable. \fBsum\fR always parses with the \fBauto\fR locale.
.CS
::tclutils::tunum::sum {1,50 2,00 4,20}          ;# -> 7.7
::tclutils::tunum::sum {1.000,00 250,50}         ;# -> 1250.5
::tclutils::tunum::sum {10 abc 5}                ;# -> 15.0  (abc skipped)
::tclutils::tunum::sum {} -default {}            ;# -> {}
.CE
.SS isNumber
.PP
Returns 1 if the value parses as a number (auto locale), else 0.
.CS
::tclutils::tunum::isNumber "3,14"   ;# -> 1
::tclutils::tunum::isNumber "x"      ;# -> 0
.CE
.SS format
.PP
Formats a number as a grouped, locale\-specific string — the rough inverse of \fBparse \-locale de\-strict\fR. Locale \fBde\fR (the only locale, and the default): dot as thousands separator, comma as decimal mark, \fB\-decimals\fR fractional digits (default 2, rounded via Tcl's \fBformat\fR).
.CS
::tclutils::tunum::format 1234.5              ;# -> 1.234,50
::tclutils::tunum::format -1234.5             ;# -> -1.234,50
::tclutils::tunum::format 1000000             ;# -> 1.000.000,00
::tclutils::tunum::format 1234.5 -decimals 0  ;# -> 1.234
::tclutils::tunum::format 1234.5 -decimals 3  ;# -> 1.234,500
.CE
.PP
For a currency string, append your own symbol/code: \fB"[::tclutils::tunum::format $n] EUR"\fR \-> \fB1.234,50 EUR\fR.
.PP
\fBformat\fR defines a command named \fBformat\fR inside the \fB::tclutils::tunum\fR namespace; it calls the Tcl builtin as \fB::format\fR internally. Callers always use the fully\-qualified \fB::tclutils::tunum::format\fR, so there is no clash.
.SS "Error codes"
.TS
l l.
errorcode	raised when
\fB{TCLUTILS TUNUM OPTION}\fR	unknown option passed to \fBparse\fR/\fBsum\fR
\fB{TCLUTILS TUNUM LOCALE}\fR	\fBparse \-locale\fR not \fBauto\fR/\fBde\-strict\fR, or \fBformat \-locale\fR not \fBde\fR
\fB{TCLUTILS TUNUM DECIMALS}\fR	\fBformat \-decimals\fR is not a non\-negative integer
.TE
.SS Notes
.IP \(bu
Output is always a Tcl double (e.g. \fB7.7\fR, not \fB"7,70"\fR). Use \fBformat\fR for display formatting, e.g. \fBformat "%.2f" [::tclutils::tunum::sum $vals]\fR.
.IP \(bu
The currency symbol \fB€\fR is handled via the \fB\eu20AC\fR escape, so the module is safe to source from a \fB.tm\fR regardless of the channel encoding.
.IP \(bu
\fBauto\fR\fB vs \fR\fBde\-strict\fR\fB:\fR \fBauto\fR is forgiving and guesses the notation; pick it for free\-form user input. \fBde\-strict\fR is deterministic and never treats a dot as a decimal mark; pick it when the input contract is strictly German.
.IP \(bu
\fBRelation to \fR\fBtclutils::tunumfmt\fR\fB:\fR the two do \fInot\fR overlap. \fBtunum\fR parses locale\-grouped / currency numbers (\fB1.234,56 €\fR); \fBtunumfmt::fromHuman\fR parses SI/IEC unit notation (\fB1.5K\fR → 1500, \fB2Mi\fR → 2097152). Use \fBtunum\fR for user\-entered amounts, \fBtunumfmt\fR for engineering / byte\-size notation.
